  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 359 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\n\nFollowing the storms this morning to early afternoon across central \nNebraska, most of the area should remain dry until at least Monday \nevening (next best widespread storm chances; 20%-30%). Sporadic and \nisolated thunderstorm activity rolling in and out of the area, \nhowever, can't be completely ruled out within a few more isolated \nlocations tonight or Sunday. Due to low run to run model \nconsistency, our confidence falls on the lower end of the spectrum \nin regards to determining exactly where and when storms may emerge. \nGiven general weak forcing and limited shear (<40kts), the potential \nfor widespread convection appears unlikely at this time. In terms of \ntonight, the best potential for any isolated storms would fall \nmainly across north central Kansas or far southern Nebraska where \nthe outflow boundary from this morning's storms resides. Even then, \nthe confidence remains low (<20% for any given location).  \n\nThe other factor that looks probable to reemerge again tonight would \nbe the potential for fog (60% chance). As with the last two nights, \ncalming winds with high surface relative humidity values \n(temperatures cooling near their dewpoints) may be just strong \nenough to help with the production of some surface condensation \n(fog). Given the higher dewpoints towards the north, the better \npotential for fog formation should fall north of I-80. The HRRR and \nother HREF members have additionally seemed to target central to \nnorth central Nebraska for fog development tonight (identified by \n60% of the HREF members). \n\nThe main story for Sunday will be the heat. Steady southerly warm air \nadvecting winds paired with mostly sunny skies will work in tandem \nto help temperatures warm to their likely warmest point of the week. \nHighs for the day will be expected to span the 90s across a majority \nof south central Nebraska. A few locations across the far southern \nrow of counties in Nebraska may even reach the triple digits (20-40% \nchances). Even warmer temperatures south of the state line will be \npossible to set up as highs spread the upper 90s to up to 105 \ndegrees across north central Kansas (20-40% chance for highs to \nreach 105 degrees). Heat index values for a majority of the area \nshould range between 100-105 degrees. Although higher amounts may be \npossible across isolated north central Kansas locations, a few \nuncertainties hinders our confidence to a degree. \n\nThe decision to hold off on the issuance of a heat advisory was well \ndiscussed this afternoon between neighboring offices. A few \nquestions have been raised about if the elevated wildfire smoke may \nlimit the solar flux as well as if the marginal dewpoints (50s to \nlow 60s) would be high enough to raise heat indices above 105 \ndegrees. The higher humidity values (mid 60s to low 70s) are forecast \nto linger across central Nebraska locations, or areas where \ntemperatures are not likely to surpass the mid 90s. Given the time \nof year and the uncertainties, the issuance of a heat advisory was \nheld off this forecast cycle.\n\nLooking ahead at monday, the next best chance for scattered \nthunderstorms returns for primarily the northern half of the area \nduring the evening to overnight hours. At the surface, a weak low \nemerging out from the Rockies may help steer another surface \nboundary down into southern Nebraska (may provide some weak \nconvergence based assent). Given the warm (90s to low 100s highs), \nmoist (mid 60s to mid 70s dewpoints) and unstable airmass (MUCAPE up \nto 3,000-5,000J/km), any storms that develops may have a shot at \nbecoming strong to severe. The only limiting factor would be from \nthe marginal shear (20-35kts). The SPC keeps most areas north of I-80 \nincluded in a marginal severe weather outlook. \n\nIn terms of the pattern aloft, the region falls inbetween broad \nridging to the west and broad troughing to the east. This leaves the \narea underneath northwest flow. Long-range forecast models continue to \nhone in on a more zonal (less amplified) pattern transitioning to a \nto slight ridging (higher pressure) pattern near the middle of next \nweek for the Central U.S. A few other notable trends that exist in \nthe long-range forecast includes a slightly wetter and cooler period \nnear the midle-to-end of the week. Highs in the forecast dip a few \ndegrees each day from Tuesday to Friday with highs likely to return \nback to the 70s (70-90% chance). Daily afternoon to evening \nthunderstorm chances additionally lie between Wednesday and Friday. \nThis wetter pattern will come as a few upper-level waves may try to \nbreak out of a Western U.S. trough.",
